    {
      "id": "s2-encoded-forbidden-source-refs",
      "criterionIds": [
        "pointers.syntax"
      ],
      "severity": "major",
      "status": "resolved",
      "descriptionMarkdown": "**Expected:** a pointer means the same thing wherever it is interpreted (design `pointer-syntax`). Source references into `resolutionOrder` or `modifiers` stay forbidden whatever their spelling.\n\n**Observed:** `Discovery::append_source` and `resolve_local_reference` check the forbidden targets on the *raw* text (`starts_with(\"#/resolutionOrder/\")` / `\"#/modifiers/\"`), but traversal now percent-decodes. A probe (deleted) showed:\n\n- a set source `{\"$ref\":\"#/%72esolutionOrder/0\"}` resolves `Ready` (it includes the first resolution step), where `#/resolutionOrder/0` is rejected;\n- `{\"$ref\":\"#/%6Dodifiers/m\"}` is accepted by `validate_complete` and fails `resolve` only with the unrelated message \"token document groups must be objects\".\n\nThe serde pointer this sprint replaced did not decode, so this bypass is new.\n\n**Repair:** run the forbidden-target checks on the decoded `pointer_parts` (first part `resolutionOrder` or `modifiers`) in `append_source` and `resolve_local_reference`. Add tests for both encoded spellings through `resolve` and `validate_complete`.\n\n**Resolution (verified):** `targets_forbidden_member` checks the decoded `pointer_parts` (`resolutionOrder`/`modifiers` with a member below it), and `resolve_local_reference` checks the decoded first part. The encoded spellings are rejected by both `resolve` and `validate_complete`.",
